WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:06.210
So number three for me, I went
with a very general thing,

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.750
but I do have some specifics.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:10.240
It was our trip to Australia and Fiji.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.060
That is my number one, okay.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:14.830
So you have hit my number one.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:17.060
Good, ha, ha, gotcha!

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:20.430
And it's that entire trip.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.840
And it's so hard.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.040
All of those moments are spectacular,

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.433
that you talked about,
and there's so many more.

00:00:29.471 --> 00:00:32.480
I'll just shout out another
one, which is when we were

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:38.454
when we went on the day trip,
you actually weren't there.

00:00:38.454 --> 00:00:39.714
The day trip outside of Sydney.

00:00:39.714 --> 00:00:41.070
Thanks for reminding me.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.920
And Lando had taken--
My son was sick.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.113
We get off, we're like
taking some pictures,

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.470
this amazing overlook, and Lando's like,

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:53.093
Dad, how do you take a selfie?

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.580
And I'm like, well son,
you just take the phone,

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:00.750
and then you hit that button
so it turns around this way,

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.360
so you can see yourself,
and then you just take it.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.220
And I'm giving it to him while he does it,

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.660
and Lando takes his first selfie.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:10.110
So that's not the number
one moment of the year,

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.360
but it's a moment that encapsulates

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.050
the type of things that
you want to happen.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:17.070
It's like, I ushered in
my son's first selfie

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.990
on an overlook in Australia.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:22.590
Now I just, it's funny
that Lando's hesitancy

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:25.040
to take pictures, let
pictures be taken of himself,

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.122
led to him being eight
or however old he was,

00:01:28.122 --> 00:01:29.110
before he took it.
Right, yes.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:32.990
I got selfies of like,
non-talking Locke and Shepherd

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:35.510
like just like picking
up the camera and...

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.536
Because he refused to
have his picture taken.

00:01:37.536 --> 00:01:38.530
And just taking pictures of themselves.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.950
He wanted to be a ghost.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.990
Now he welcomes pictures
being taken of him.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:47.600
I mean, again, that's
another facet of that memory

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.600
is that he's changed and developed

00:01:52.858 --> 00:01:54.650
in terms of his self-confidence.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:56.400
That like there's all
these things wrapped up

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.860
into this goofy selfie that he took.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:02.180
Where he's like,

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.773
he's got that funny
selfie look on his face.

00:02:06.531 --> 00:02:10.140
So yeah, I mean, that whole thing,

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.900
that whole trip is my number one.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:16.550
And it was just, man, we've carved out

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:20.723
time in 2019 for us to
have extended vacations.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:23.420
And we're kinda earmarking
some of that time,

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:24.660
and trying to figure it out.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.550
So I'm, it actually led to that.

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:30.480
Like, okay, we can, let's go big.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:31.313
Well, I think.

00:02:31.313 --> 00:02:33.359
I think carve out some time so that,

00:02:33.359 --> 00:02:34.192
to create more of those.

00:02:34.192 --> 00:02:36.643
Well, it shows you that,
I mean, first of all,

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.900
one of the great things about travel,

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.680
is because it shakes
up your normal routine,

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.740
it tends to be the thing that you remember

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:47.340
when you look back,

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.670
because it broke you
out of your day-to-day.

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:51.110
Right.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:54.460
I even heard someone say,
man, this was on a podcast.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:56.470
I'm sorry, I cannot remember who said it,

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:59.370
but it was great advice
that someone's dad said.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:00.630
Giving financial advice.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:04.060
That there's only a few things
you should go into debt for,

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:06.510
but one of those should be vacation.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:11.100
So like don't, you can
justify going into debt

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.290
for a family vacation, which yeah,

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.060
the eyebrows raise a little bit, right?

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:15.937
But it's like--

00:03:15.937 --> 00:03:17.010
Well that's a provocative thing to say.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.020
It is.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:19.260
And that's why he said it.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:22.710
But the thing that I have
thought about this year is

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:26.580
when I think about what
I'm spending my money on.

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:28.905
It wasn't the only,
there was another thing.

00:03:28.905 --> 00:03:32.380
I have thought that like, this feels,

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:35.040
if we're paying for a trip, for paying to,

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.740
I've got some exciting
stuff I'll talk about

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.820
later 'cause it's not happening time soon.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:43.930
But paying for travel in my mind is,

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:48.450
I'm not gonna go into debt
for it, but it's justified.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.050
I'm like, hmm, you look at the price tag,

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:51.860
but you're like yeah, but
I'm creating this memory.

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:53.950
So yeah, I think it's definitely.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:57.260
I don't know what your number
one is, better be good.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:00.623
Well, my number one is therapy.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:03.170
Is that it?
Yeah.

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:06.640
Now therapy for me did not start in 2018.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:10.410
It started the very,
very tail end of 2017.

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:11.243
Okay.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:14.520
And I've talked about it a little bit.

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:18.933
But the thing, I mean, again,

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:23.700
I'm self-conscious
anytime I talk about it,

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:26.230
'cause I feel like it's one of those,

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:28.260
it's one of those really
LA things to talk about.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.240
And I know that we're out
here in LA in this bubble

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:31.920
and you can kind of forget what

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:36.770
it feels like to not be here.

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:38.240
Yeah.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:42.610
But, interestingly, I
would say that, again,

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:45.840
it's kind of very similar
to what I was talking about

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.840
earlier that, you know, you
change so significantly,

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:53.793
so quickly, when you're growing up.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:56.930
And you're going through,

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:59.940
you think about how quickly our children

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:02.270
are changing as they grow up.

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:04.470
And like, we haven't changed that much.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:07.740
You change in these dramatic ways,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.880
both physically, but
your brain is changing,

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:12.850
and then the way you see
the world is changing.

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:15.790
And then even in your,
during your college time,

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:20.053
in your 20s, you're
experiencing these just changes.

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:24.350
In, even in your personality,

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:26.240
not fundamentally your personality,

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:27.270
but just different aspects.

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:31.470
And I kinda feel like in a lot of ways

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:34.740
up until I started kind
of exploring these things

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:37.090
with the help of a professional,

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:42.090
that I kind of just assumed I
was going to kinda just be me.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:43.760
Just be the same guy.
Yeah, yeah.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.030
For the rest of your life.

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:47.230
I'm like, I'm pretty healthy in general.

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:49.530
I mean, I originally went to therapy,

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:52.600
not because I thought that there
was something wrong with me

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.640
in terms of like the way that I see myself

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:01.580
or think about myself, I
went because of the stress,

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:02.987
what stress was doing to me.

00:06:02.987 --> 00:06:05.740
The fact that I kinda let stress affect me

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:09.240
to the point that it was
affecting me physically.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.480
And I was like, I just need
somebody to help me deal,

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:13.420
like kind of deal with this.

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:15.360
I got help with that.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:20.040
But I think that the sort
of the breakthrough for me,

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:21.770
or the beginning of a breakthrough,

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:24.250
is when I started beginning to understand

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.720
why I am the way that I
am, like why my personality

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:28.790
is the way that I am, the way that it is.

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:33.790
Why I'm so independent, and why I can cry

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:38.940
while watching a commercial.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:40.380
The "Lion King" trailer.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:43.360
Like literally can, I started watching,

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.550
first of all, if you're not
watching "Dogs" on Netflix,

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:48.870
I don't know what you're doing.

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:51.040
You should watch "Dogs" on Netflix.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:54.550
It's a documentary
series where they profile

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:57.450
a different dog and human
relationship in every one.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:59.333
And I literally, the first episode,

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:03.010
started crying during the opening credits.

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:05.017
And then I just cried the
whole time every episode.

00:07:05.017 --> 00:07:06.210
Is that when they're sacrificing cats,

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:07.560
like when the opening credits?

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:10.700
And speaking of cats, let
me just say right now,

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:13.780
you could not make this
documentary about cats.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:14.650
Okay?
Hold on.

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:16.330
Now hold on, we're talking
about your therapy.

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:17.163
Let's get back to you.

00:07:17.163 --> 00:07:18.250
You couldn't make it!

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:20.630
Don't deflect into the world of cat hate.

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:21.690
I, that was my fault.

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:24.480
In fact, I challenge you
to make a documentary

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:27.200
about the bond between cats and people,

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:28.720
and all the wonderful things these...

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:30.370
Watch "Dogs", and then tell me

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:31.810
that they could do the
same thing with cats.

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:32.643
They couldn't!

00:07:32.643 --> 00:07:34.250
It's impossible!
Unless you're about to cry

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:35.600
let's bring it back to you.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:39.873
I'm in therapy for
this, for my cat hatred.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:46.730
But, it's interesting
because I cry really easily

00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:47.570
at certain things like that.

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:50.240
But then if you ask me things about,

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:53.990
and I had a very, I had a great childhood,

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:58.260
I didn't experience like trauma.

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:02.030
And, but if you ask me
about things that like,

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:04.900
the most difficult things
that I experienced growing up

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:06.942
or whatever, I don't, there's no,

00:08:06.942 --> 00:08:09.080
I don't experience, I don't, I won't cry.

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:11.380
I don't have sadness about those things.

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:15.800
But I can tell a story about
'em and make somebody else cry.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:19.010
But yet, so I started
learning that, like I,

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:21.430
you got, your emotions have
to come out in some way.

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:25.310
And so for me, I had found
this way to kinda deal with 'em

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:29.160
by like I'm gonna watch
this documentary about dogs.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:32.246
But yet not deal with
what's going on inside

00:08:32.246 --> 00:08:35.683
and kind of, it's more locked up.

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:40.030
That's just kind of the
surface level talking about it.

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:43.370
But anyway, therapy has
been significant for me

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:47.250
because it represents
sort of the beginning of

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:50.180
a sort of a second journey in my life

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:55.180
of kinda going inside and
figuring some things out.

00:08:56.704 --> 00:08:59.163
I feel like I'm at the
very beginning of that.

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:01.500
And there's a lot of work.

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:02.730
You hear people about talking

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:04.770
about the work that they're doing.

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:06.720
And then you start figuring out like, oh,

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:08.880
I need to be working
on myself in this way.

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:11.440
And it's not just about some
of the more obvious things,

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:13.040
which we've we've both been doing,

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:16.220
which is like getting
our schedule in order,

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:18.330
and making sure that we've
got things for the kids.

00:09:18.330 --> 00:09:20.490
Which some of that has been
really assisted by therapy.

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:22.640
But the like, what is it about me?

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:26.220
How can I continue to grow?

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:27.870
And then how does that then affect

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:28.920
the relationships that I have?

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:30.887
How does that affect how,
what kind of father I am,

00:09:30.887 --> 00:09:33.120
and what kind of husband I
am, what kind of friend I am?

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:38.120
So that's been, that's number one.

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:40.480
No specific moment.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:42.380
I won't talk about it
like a moment in therapy

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:45.627
in which I had like a
breakthrough, I'll keep that to me.

00:09:45.627 --> 00:09:46.460
That's your private list.

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:47.470
Between me and my therapist.

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:49.627
But therapy as a whole.

